  "summaries": [
    {
      "id": 927397,
      "category": "team",
      "content": "A flaw in Node.js TLS hostname verification can cause a server certificate to be validated against a different IP address than the one the client connected to, when the host is a non-canonical IPv4 literal with a leading zero, such as `0127.0.0.1`.\n\n`tls.checkServerIdentity()` normalizes the host following the WHATWG URL rules, which read a leading-zero octet as octal (`0127.0.0.1` becomes `87.0.0.1`), while the connection is made through the operating system resolver, which on some platforms such as macOS reads it as decimal (`127.0.0.1`). A server at the connected address can then be authenticated with a certificate issued for another IP address.\n\nThis vulnerability affects Node.js **26.x**, **24.x**, and **22.x**.",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-23T14:49:02.558Z",
